  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Birch jelly (Exidia repanda) is a gelatinous fungus belonging to the Auriculariaceae family, characterized by its translucent, jelly-like fruiting bodies. It commonly appears on dead or dying hardwood, particularly birch trees, throughout New England.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Gelatinous texture",
      "description": "Firm yet jelly-like consistency that becomes soft and watery when wet; somewhat rubbery when dry",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Color and appearance",
      "description": "Translucent to whitish or pale brown, often with an undulating or brain-like surface texture",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Host preference",
      "description": "Strongly associated with birch wood, though occasionally found on other hardwoods; grows in clusters or rosettes",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Birch jelly thrives on dead or weakened hardwood logs and branches, particularly birch species, in moist woodlands and shaded areas throughout New England.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Despite its gel-like appearance, birch jelly is edible and has been used in traditional Asian cuisine and folk medicine for centuries, though it is relatively tasteless and valued more for its texture than flavor.",
